@@ -0,0 +1,131 @@
+---
+id: "0118"
+status: accepted
+date: 2026-09-21
+---
+
+# ADR 0118: SF Guardrail supports an optional local Jev risk provider
+
+## Context
+
+SF Guardrail's structural and native-tool rules establish a useful baseline, but
+semantic risk can appear through unfamiliar syntax, indirect mutation, missing
+org or browser facts, and misleading claims of prior approval. Simple Jev can
+score two choices with a local model using selected next-token logits. Replacing
+semantic detection requires evidence of equal or better safety and usability;
+it must preserve the user's exact policy and SF Pi's approval authority.
+
+The previous editing-rule non-goal prohibited any model-dependent Guardrail
+decision. This decision replaces that specific prohibition for an optional,
+local, qualified semantic risk provider. It preserves the Safety Mediator
+posture in [ADR 0033](./0033-sf-guardrail-is-a-safety-mediator.md), the approval
+boundaries in [ADR 0034](./0034-sf-guardrail-approvals-use-safety-envelopes.md),
+and [ADR 0039](./0039-sf-guardrail-exposes-no-llm-tools.md)'s prohibition on
+agent-callable approval or policy tools.
+
+## Decision
+
+The existing SF Guardrail `tool_call` hook is the sole enforcement owner. Jev
+supplies a versioned risk judgment through a provider-discovery interface. It
+cannot prompt for approval, grant an approval, change operator policy, or execute
+the original request. Approval copy and enforcement stay deterministic; no LLM
+command explainer is introduced.
+
+An operator selects `off`, `shadow`, or `enforce` using
+`SF_GUARDRAIL_JEV_MODE` before launch. The default is `off`. Shadow mode records
+comparisons while preserving the current engine's prompts, approval reuse, and
+execution outcomes. Enforcement requires one warmed provider and a passing
+qualification receipt bound to its physical model, native scorer, scoring
+protocol, and installed SF risk implementation. Failed
+qualification keeps the existing engine active.
+
+For an eligible request, the model receives the complete original request and
+independently resolved org observations. The host excludes the
+current engine's classifications, reasons, and risk labels. The model scores
+safe-without-approval versus approval-required using selected next-token logits.
+Scores are uncalibrated.
+Safe judgments become `allow`; risky and valid uncertain judgments become
+`confirm`. Exact blocking policy becomes `block` in code.
+
+Protected paths, hard blocks, explicit command-rule overrides, validated cleanup
+exceptions, and tool execution-intent checks remain authoritative. Known native
+mutation confirmations also remain a host floor: an apparently read-only
+Anonymous Apex body can call org code with side effects, so direct-DML lexical
+checks cannot safely waive its existing confirmation. Custom policy must not be
+weakened by a model. Model-derived approvals bind the full original operation,
+host facts, resolved org identity, cwd, effective policy, model
+checksum, scoring protocol, and installed risk implementation. Novel semantic
+risks receive exact operation scopes rather than broad grants. Only an existing
+bounded, non-production approval scope may permit session reuse; a new semantic
+shell risk does not gain a session-wide approval. Changes to those identities
+cannot reuse an older model approval.
+
+Missing models, unavailable or duplicate providers, invalid or incomplete input
+and predictions, cancellation, changed provider identity, and timeouts use the
+current engine's decision and record the fallback. Changed or unavailable
+browser reference evidence can instead require a fresh, exact confirmation;
+it cannot silently become an allow. This is an operational fallback, not a
+safe model answer. Browser clicks and key presses remain on the current engine
+in both shadow and enforce modes because a cached snapshot cannot prove the
+current page, reference, or focus when the browser action executes. They require
+live facts and revalidation immediately before execution before model scoring
+can be eligible. Warm risk checks have a hard 750 ms deadline, including
+preparation and queueing; the ideal warm p95 is below 500 ms. Cold
+initialization is measured separately. Module
+load and factory registration start no model worker; Jev owns and disposes its
+dedicated worker on shutdown and disable. Existing commands expose mode,
+readiness, warmup, comparisons, and fallback reasons.
+
+## Qualification
+
+Train a separate guardrail candidate from the reviewed Google Gemma 3 1B base
+with the existing RFDT pipeline. Keep the default general classifier unchanged.
+Qwen and Chinese-lineage models, derivatives, teachers, and fallbacks remain
+excluded. Artifact review proves provenance and execution eligibility; it does
+not prove semantic safety.
+
+A dedicated operation-policy rubric labels shell and `herdr_pane` commands,
+protected files, org-sensitive Salesforce operations, Apex, Agent Script,
+Data 360, SOQL disclosure, Slack Canvas, and browser commits. Run the current
+engine independently to establish the baseline. Disagreement with it is not
+automatically a candidate error.
+
+Keep related variants in the same split. Reserve separate training, validation,
+and held-out groups that cover unfamiliar syntax, indirect mutations, harmless
+quoted text, misleading approval claims, and missing or stale facts. Select the
+candidate using validation only. Freeze weights, prompt, cutoff, and
+qualification criteria before the held-out test. Reject failed candidates; do
+not adjust the criteria using held-out results.
+
+| Criterion                           | Required evidence                                                              |
+| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| Unsafe automatic allows             | Zero on the frozen qualification corpus.                                       |
+| Existing safety protections         | No regression relative to the current engine.                                  |
+| Exact hard blocks                   | No weakening or bypass.                                                        |
+| Unnecessary confirmations or blocks | At or below the current engine's baseline.                                     |
+| Warm risk-check latency             | p95 at or below 500 ms, including preparation and queueing.                    |
+| Hard warm deadline                  | No check exceeds 750 ms, including preparation and queueing.                   |
+| Model execution                     | Every eligible qualification call completes; fallback cannot conceal failures. |
+
+Parity supports an “as effective” claim. Improvement requires the same evaluation
+to demonstrate additional correctly detected risks or fewer unnecessary
+interruptions. Integration tests, corpus results, matched workflow results, and
+production acceptance remain separate claims. The existence of this provider
+interface or ADR is not model qualification.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Exact policy and the existing approval, session, revocation, and audit flow
+  stay inspectable and remain in SF Pi.
+- Shadow mode provides a comparison path before model enforcement is eligible.
+- A failed or unavailable model preserves the current engine and leaves visible
+  evidence of the failure.
+- A dedicated worker isolates guardrail checks from Jev's general classifier
+  queue and allows explicit warmup and disposal.
+- Qualification must exercise the real Pi hook with stubbed tools, plus
+  cancellation, recovery, malformed predictions, provider discovery failures,
+  tampered receipts, policy/model changes, revoked approvals, and headless
+  behavior. Dangerous external operations are unnecessary for these proofs.
+- Matched workflow evidence includes accepted outcomes, confirmations, retries,
+  and elapsed time. Passing a finite corpus does not establish production
+  acceptance or safety on every possible operation.
